2 CONT
2 CONT Galindo Aznárez II (died 922) was Count of Aragón (893-922), the son and successor of Aznar Galíndez II.
2 CONT
2 CONT Galindo was one of a coalition that sponsored the 905 coup d'état in Pamplona in favor of his brother-in-law, Sancho I of Pamplona. However, he turned on this new king and in 911 attacked him in concert with brother-in-law Muhamma
2 CONC d al-Tawil and Abd Allah ibn Lubb ibn Qasi. This coalition was defeated, al-Tawil killed, and Galindo forced to become vassal of Sancho.
2 CONT
2 CONT Galindo was married twice. By his first wife, Acibella Garcés of Gascony, daughter of Count García II Sánchez of Gascony, he had sons Miro and Bishop Redemtus, along with daughter Toda, wife of Hunifred Bernat, count of Ribagorza,
2 CONC  to whom she brought Sobrarbe. By his second wife, Sancha Garcés of Pamplona, daughter of García Jiménez and sister of Sancho I, he had Velasquita and Andregota Galíndez, who married García Sanchez, king of Pamplona (925-970). He 
2 CONC also sired several illegitimate sons: Guntoslo, Sancho, Belasco, Banzo, and Aznar. The first of these, Guntoslo, is apparently the man of this name who would later be count of Aragon, but only as a fully subservient vassal of his 
2 CONC brother-in-law the king of Pamplona.
2 CONT
2 CONT Preceded by
2 CONT
2 CONT Aznar Galíndez II 	Count of Aragon
2 CONT
2 CONT 893–922 	Succeeded by
2 CONT
2 CONT Andregota Galíndez
2 CONT
2 CONT This page was last modified on 17 April 2010 at 10:31.
