1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Banu Qasi 
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT The Banu Qasi, Banu Kasi, Beni Casi (Arabic: بنو قسي‎, meaning "sons" or "heirs of Cassius") or Banu Musa were a Basque Muladi dynasty that ruled the upper Ebro valley in the 9th, before being displaced in the first quarter of the
2 CONC  10th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Leadership of the Banu Qasi
2 CONT
2 CONT The following men are the documented leaders of the Banu Qasi (entried in italics are of uncertain affiliation to the family): 
2 CONT
2 CONT Cassius, fl. 714 
2 CONT
2 CONT Abu Taur, Wali of Huesca, fl. 778, perhaps son of Cassius 
2 CONT
2 CONT Musa ibn Fortun, (perh. assassinated 788), grandson of Cassius 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mutarrif ibn Musa, assassinated 799, perhaps son of Musa ibn Fortun 
2 CONT
2 CONT Fortun ibn Musa, assassinated 801, perhaps son of Musa ibn Fortun, else identical to him 
2 CONT
2 CONT Musa ibn Musa, d. 862, son of Musa ibn Fortun 
2 CONT
2 CONT Lubb ibn Musa, d. 875, son of Musa ibn Musa 
2 CONT
2 CONT Is'mail ibn Musa, co-leader to 882, d. 889, son of Musa ibn Musa 
2 CONT
2 CONT Muhammad ibn Lubb, co-leader to 882, then sole leader, d. 899, son of Lubb ibn Musa 
2 CONT
2 CONT Lubb ibn Muhammad, d. 907, son of Muhammad ibn Lubb 
2 CONT
2 CONT Abd Allah ibn Muhammad, d. 915, son of Muhammad ibn Lubb 
2 CONT
2 CONT (succession struggle between Mutarrif ibn Muhammad and Muhammad ibn Abd Allah, 915-916) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Muhammad ibn Abd Allah, d. 923, son of Abd Allah ibn Muhammad 
2 CONT
2 CONT Muhammad ibn Lubb, d. 929, son of Lubb ibn Muhammad 
2 CONT
2 CONT (end of dynasty)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT < [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garc%C3%ADa_Íñiguez_of_Pamplona Wikipedia] > García Íñiguez of Pamplona
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho Garcés, father of Aznar Sánchez de Larraun — the second husband of his cousin Onneca Fortúnez — and probably of Velazquita, who married '''Mutarrif ibn Musa, of the Banu Qasi.'''[a]
2 CONT
2 CONT a.  Chronicler Ibn Hayyan says Velasquita was daughter of king García Íñiguez, but al-Udri names her father as Sancho, lord of Pamplona.[10] The latter has been preferred by historians.[9][11] This reference to Sancho as 'lord', a
2 CONC long with a reference by Ibn Khaldun to a Sancho, 'governor of Pamplona and chief of the Basques' in 865, and an 867 charter that names 'king' Sancho as son-in-law of Galindo Aznárez I of Aragón, led Antonio Ubieto Arteta to propo
2 CONC se that it was Sancho Garcés who ruled in Pamplona in the mid-to-late 860s, during at least part of the period that his brother, Fortún Garcés, was held captive in Córdoba.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/MOORISH%20SPAIN.htm#_Toc371931142
2 CONT
2 CONT * MUSA ibn Musa, son of MUSA ibn Fortun & his second wife --- (-Tudela 26 Sep 862). … Musa had [seven] children by unknown [wives/concubines]: 
2 CONT ** 3.         '''MUTARIF ibn Musa''' (-crucified Córdoba 5 Sep 873). Al-Udri records that "en algunos Anales" record that "Musa ibn Musa" was survived by "varios hijos…Lubb, Ismail, Mutarrif y Fortun"[467].  Al-Udri records that "
2 CONC Mutarrif ibn Musa" took control of Huesca after Amrus ibn Umar was expelled, dated to A.H. 257 [870/71], an earlier passage recording, as a variation, that "Lubb [ibn Musa]" granted "el mando…de Huesca" to "su hermano…Mutarrif" af
2 CONC ter his campaign in A.H. 258 [871/72][468].  He captured Tudela in Dec 871, but was captured by Emir Muhammad who had him executed along with his sons[469].  Al-Udri records that "Fortun ibn Musa" rebelled but was captured by "el 
2 CONC imam Muhammad" who imprisoned him and "Mutarrif ibn Musa…en la prisión de al-Duwayra en Córdoba"[470].  Al-Udri records that "Mutarrif ibn Musa" was crucified in Córdoba "en el Llano de Mutarrif, el viernes 8 de du l´qada" A.H. 25
2 CONC 9 [5 Sep 873][471].  m (x) secondly (870) VELASQUITA Sánchez, daughter of [SANCHO García de Pamplona & his wife ---].  Al-Udri records that "Mutarrif ibn Musa" married "Faliskita, hija de Sanyo, señor de Pamplona" and that he brou
2 CONC ght her to Huesca (which suggests that the marriage should be dated to [870/71] when Mutarif took control of Huesca) and had children by her[472].  Mutarif had six children by unknown mothers: 
2 CONT *** a)         MUHAMMAD ibn Mutarif (-crucified Córdoba 5 Sep 873).  Ibn Hazm names "Muhammad, Musa y Lubb, todos tres crucificados en Córdoba junto con su padre y al mismo tiempo…Yusuf…Abd Allah e Ismail" as the sons of "Mutarrif
2 CONC  [ibn Musa] el que fue crucificado"[473].  
2 CONT *** b)         MUSA ibn Mutarif (-crucified Córdoba 5 Sep 873).  Ibn Hazm names "Muhammad, Musa y Lubb, todos tres crucificados en Córdoba junto con su padre y al mismo tiempo…Yusuf…Abd Allah e Ismail" as the sons of "Mutarrif [ib
2 CONC n Musa] el que fue crucificado"[474].  
2 CONT *** c)         LUBB ibn Mutarif (-crucified Córdoba 5 Sep 873).  Ibn Hazm names "Muhammad, Musa y Lubb, todos tres crucificados en Córdoba junto con su padre y al mismo tiempo…Yusuf…Abd Allah e Ismail" as the sons of "Mutarrif [ib
2 CONC n Musa] el que fue crucificado"[475].  
2 CONT *** d)         YUSUF ibn Mutarif (-Nájera --).  Ibn Hazm names "Muhammad, Musa y Lubb, todos tres crucificados en Córdoba junto con su padre y al mismo tiempo…Yusuf…Abd Allah e Ismail" as the sons of "Mutarrif [ibn Musa] el que fu
2 CONC e crucificado", adding that Yusuf was killed "en Nájera"[476].  
2 CONT *** e)         ABD ALLAH ibn Mutarif .  Ibn Hazm names "Muhammad, Musa y Lubb, todos tres crucificados en Córdoba junto con su padre y al mismo tiempo…Yusuf…Abd Allah e Ismail" as the sons of "Mutarrif [ibn Musa] el que fue crucif
2 CONC icado", adding that Abd Allah and Ismail converted to christianity[477].  
2 CONT *** f)          ISMAIL ibn Mutarif .  Ibn Hazm names "Muhammad, Musa y Lubb, todos tres crucificados en Córdoba junto con su padre y al mismo tiempo…Yusuf…Abd Allah e Ismail" as the sons of "Mutarrif [ibn Musa] el que fue crucific
2 CONC ado", adding that Abd Allah and Ismail converted to christianity[478]. '''Mutarif & his wife (x) had [two] children''':  
2 CONT *** g)         [two] children ([872/early 874]-).  Al-Udri records that "Mutarrif ibn Musa" married "Faliskita, hija de Sanyo, señor de Pamplona" and that he brought her to Huesca (which suggests that the marriage should be dated 
2 CONC to [870/71] when Mutarif took control of Huesca) and had children by her (but gives no details about these children)[479].  There appears to be time between the suggested date of the marriage of their parents, and their father´s d
2 CONC eath, for two children to have been born, the second maybe born posthumously. 
