1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Íñigo Garcés was the eldest son of García Jiménez by his first wife, Oneca Rebelle de Sangüesa. His father was of "another part of the realm", and thought to have been some sort of ruler, and Íñigo may have succeed
2 CONC ed his father. He appears with the royal title in the Códice de Roda, but only in a secondary reference, not in his primary entry. When the 905 coup d'état placed his younger half-brother Sancho Garcés on the throne, he or his fam
2 CONC ily may have had a falling out, as one son was killed while other sons fled into exile.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married a Jimena Velázquez, granddaughter of king Fortún Garcés of Pamplona, and by her had a daughter, Toda, wife of García Íñiguez of Olza, and four sons, García, killed at Liedena, and Jimeno, Fortún and Sancho, who took ref
2 CONC uge in Córdoba.
2 CONT
2 CONT [source: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Íñigo_Garcés_of_Pamplona]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Íñigo Garcés of Pamplona
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Íñigo Garcés was the eldest son of García Jiménez by his first wife, Oneca Rebelle de Sangüesa. His father was a "king" of "another part of the realm", and Íñigo may have succeeded his father. He appears with the royal title in th
2 CONC e Códice de Roda, but only in a secondary reference, not in his primary entry. When the 905 coup d'état placed his younger half-brother Sancho Garcés on the throne, he or his family may have had a falling out, as one son was kille
2 CONC d while other sons fled into exile.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married a Jimena Velasquez, granddaughter of king Fortún, and by her had a daughter, Toda, wife of García Íñiguez of Olza, and four sons, García, killed at Liedena, and Jimeno, Fortún and Sancho, who took refuge in Córdoba.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%8D%C3%B1igo_Garc%C3%A9s_of_Pamplona
2 CONT
2 CONT Íñigo Garcés of Pamplona
