1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT < [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bermudo_Núñez Wikipedia] > '''Bermudo Núñez''' (died c. 955) was a magnate from León and the first Count of Cea. He appears for the first time in 921 confirming a donation to the Monastery of San B
2 CONC enito in Sahagún from which it can be assumed that he was probably born near the end of the 9th century.[1] A loyal vassal of the Leonese monarchs, he confirmed several royal charters and appears with the title of Count from at le
2 CONC ast the year 950, governing as a tenente the Cea region from 939 onwards.
2 CONT
2 CONT He probably played a relevant role in the Battle of Simancas and after this victory of the Leonese troops, collaborated with his brother, Oveco Núñez, Bishop of León in the repoblación of Salamanca.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Family origins
2 CONT
2 CONT Although his filiation has not been confirmed, several hypotheses have been put forward. Medievalist scholar Margarita Torres Sevilla-Quiñones de León believes that he could have been the son of Nuño Ordóñez, a son of King Ordoño 
2 CONC I of Asturias and brother of Alfonso III,[3] which, according to the author, would explain the rapid rise of this lineage and its proximity to the royal house.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to another hypothesis, proposed by genealogist Jaime de Salazar y Acha, Bermudo would have been the son of a Nuño Vélaz (or Vela), a son of Vela Jiménez, Count of Álava, through whom the Vela lineage spread throughout th
2 CONC e Kingdom of León which would also explain the Basque-Navarrese names in subsequent generations.[4][a]
2 CONT
2 CONT Bishop Oveco Núñez in a charter dated 28 August 945, confirms several donations made by King Ramiro II of León to the Monastery of Sahagún, also confirming the charter his brothers Vela, Suero, Munio, Bermudo and Nuño Núñez, follo
2 CONC wed by some of the children of these brothers, including Fernando Bermúdez, son of Bermudo Núñez.[6] He also had a sister named Gontrodo who married Ansur Fernández, Count of Monzón, the parents of Queen Teresa Ansúrez, wife of Ki
2 CONC ng Sancho I of León.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Count Bermudo Núñez married twice.[8] From his first marriage to Argilo, he was the father of:
2 CONT * Froiloba Bermúdez, the first-born, married Muño Flaínez before 947.
2 CONT * Fernando Bermúdez (died in 978), the second Count of Cea, inherited from his father and from his uncle Bishop Oveco.
2 CONT * Piniolo Bermúdez, who confirms, with his brothers Fernando and Jimena, a donation made by his father on 13 August 949 to the Monastery of Santiago de Valdávida.
2 CONT * Jimena Bermúdez
2 CONT * Vela Bermúdez (died after March 976), the father of Argilo, Nuño, and of Count Bermudo Vela.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 947 and 949, he married Velasquita, with whom he had two sons:
2 CONT * Álvaro Bermúdez
2 CONT * Oveco Bermúdez
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT a.  The name Vela and its various patronymics is of Visigothic origin.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Genealogy 
2 CONT < [https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SPANISH%20NOBILITY%20EARLY%20MEDIEVAL.htm#PedroFlainezMBronildeB Medlands] > 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''MUNIO Flaínez''', son of FLAÍN Díaz & his wife --- (-[31 Oct 961/19 Jun 962]).  "Osorio Monniz, Monnio Gudesteiz, Diaco Romaniz, Monnio Flainiz, Veremudus Flainiz" confirmed the charter dated 1 Jan 943 under which King Ramiro I
2 CONC I granted to “Vermudo Nunniz” property confiscated from “Dom Patre”[962].  …  m (before 13 Aug 947) '''FROILOBA Vermúdez''', daughter of '''VERMUDO Núñez Conde en Cea''' & '''his first wife Argila --''' . …
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bermudo_Núñez '''Bibliography'''
2 CONT ** Calleja Puerta, Miguel (2001). El conde Suero Vermúdez, su parentela y su entorno social: La aristocracia asturleonesa en los siglos XI y XII (in Spanish). Oviedo: KRK Ediciones. ISBN 84-95401-68-1.
2 CONT ** Justi, Karl (1999). Velázquez y su siglo (in Spanish). Madrid: Ediciones Istmo. ISBN 84-7090-364-0.
2 CONT ** Salazar y Acha, Jaime de (1985). "Una Familia de la Alta Edad Media: Los Velas y su Realidad Histórica". Estudios Genealógicos y Heráldicos (in Spanish). Madrid: Asociación Española de Estudios Genealógicos y Heráldicos. ISBN 8
2 CONC 4-398-3591-4.
2 CONT ** Torres Sevilla-Quiñones de León, Margarita Cecilia (1999). Linajes nobiliarios de León y Castilla. Siglos IX-XIII (in Spanish). Salamanca: Junta de Castilla y León, Consejería de educación y cultura. ISBN 84-7846-781-5.
