1 NOTE {geni:about_me} --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Leader of the galaico-portuguese nobility.  Tutor of Fernando II of Leon and Galicia. Count of Trava and Trastamara.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Fernando (or Fernán) Pérez de Traba''' (Spanish: [feɾˈnando ˈpeɾeθ ðe ˈtɾaβa, feɾˈnam -]; c. 1090 – 1 November 1155), or Fernão Peres de Trava (Portuguese: [fɨɾˈnɐ̃w ˈpeɾɨʒ ðɨ ˈtɾavɐ]), was a nobleman and count of the Kingdom o
2 CONC f León who for a time held power over all Galicia. He became the lover of Countess Teresa of Portugal, through whom he attained great influence in that domain, and was the de facto ruler of the County of Portugal between 1121 and 
2 CONC 1128.[2][3] The Poema de Almería, a Latin poem celebrating one of Alfonso VII's major victories of the Reconquista, records that "if one were to see him [Fernán], one would judge him already a king."[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Fernán was the second son of Pedro Fróilaz de Traba, founder of the House of Traba, and his first wife, Urraca Fróilaz.[5] His family was the most powerful in Galicia at the time, and he himself held properties in the most importa
2 CONC nt Galician cities: Lugo and Santiago de Compostela.[6][7] Fernán's first appearance in the surviving documentation dates from September 1107, just after the death of Raymond of Galicia, when his father confirmed a privilege of Al
2 CONC fonso VI for the monastery of Caaveiro, along with his sons.[a]
2 CONT
2 CONT Early in the twelfth century (before 1125), Pedro gave his son a Moorish cook, probably a slave, with the Christian name Martin.[10] Sometime early in the century Fernán took a wife, but they were separated when he became the love
2 CONC r of Theresa Alfónsez, Countess of Portugal. With Teresa he had two daughters: 
2 CONT *Sancha (born c. 1121), who married Álvaro Rodríguez, and 
2 CONT *Teresa, who first married Nuño Pérez de Lara and, when widowed, became the second wife of King Ferdinand II of León. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Fernán's only attested wife, Sancha González, daughter of Gonzalo Ansúrez and Urraca Vermúdez, was therefore possibly his second wife. The earliest record of their marriage is from 1134.[2] With her the count had three children: 
2 CONT *Gonzalo, 
2 CONT *María (married Ponce de Cabrera), and 
2 CONT *Urraca, the wife of Juan Arias. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancha was still living on 24 July 1161, when she signed a document, noting that she was a widow.[11][12] Probably in that same year she drew up her will. It is preserved, albeit with an incorrect date, in the cartulary Tumbo C of
2 CONC  Santiago de Compostela, since the archbishop-elect of Santiago, Fernando Curialis, was a beneficiary.[13]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fernando_Pérez_de_Traba
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Fernando Pérez de Traba
2 CONT
2 CONT b. circa 1100
2 CONT
2 CONT Father	conde de Traba Pedro Fróilaz de Traba1 b. circa 1090
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Fernando Pérez de Traba was a distinguished Galician noble who played a vital role in twelfth-century Peninsular politics. He was born circa 1100. He was the son of conde de Traba Pedro Fróilaz de Traba. Fernando Pérez de Traba as
2 CONC sumed a commanding position in the Portuguese court following the death of Count Henry, by reason of his intimate relationship with Henry's wife, Teresa before 1128. He was a witness where Afonso I "O Conquistador", rei de Portuga
2 CONC l acquired control of Portugal from his mother, who had been regent for him, in 1128 at the Battle of São Mamede, near Guimarães, Braga, Portugal. Fernando Pérez de Traba fled to the court of Alfonso VII of Castile following his b
2 CONC anishment from Portugal in 1128. He married Sancha González before 1154.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancha González b. circa 1131
2 CONT
2 CONT Child	
2 CONT
2 CONT Teresa Fernández de Traba b. c 1154, d. 7 Feb 1180
2 CONT
