1 OCCU seigneur de Montpellier, Herre i Montpellier, Seigneur de Montpellier, Señor de Montpellier (ca.1172-1202)
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} -https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_VIII_of_Montpellier
2 CONT
2 CONT -https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/TOULOUSE%20NOBILITY.htm#GuillaumeVIIIdied1218<br/>
2 CONT GUILLAUME [VIII] de Montpellier, son of GUILLAUME [VII] Seigneur de Montpellier & his wife Mathilde de Bourgogne (-Sep 1203).  Under his testament dated 29 Sep 1172, "Guillelmus dominus Montispessulani, filius quondam Sibilie" nam
2 CONC es "Guillelmo filio meo majori" as his principal heir[860].  Seigneur de Montpellier.  "Guillelmus dominus Montispessulani et…Guido eius patruus atque tutor" recognised the rights of the church of Maguelone over "mons Sancti Baudi
2 CONC lii" by charter dated May 1173[861].  "Guillelmus dominus Montis pessulani filius Matildis" swore allegiance to the bishop of Maguelone by charter dated Apr 1184[862].  "Guillelmus…Montispessulani, filius quondam Mathildis ducisse
2 CONC " disinherited his oldest daughter Marie on her second marriage in Dec 1197 in favour of his oldest son by his second (bigamous) marriage[863].  "Raimundus de Rocafolio et…Guillelma uxor ejus que vocor Marchisia" acknowledged paym
2 CONC ent by "Guillemus dominus Montispessulani, filius quondam Mathildis ducisse" of the dowry promised to her, by charter dated Oct 1200[864].  The abbot of Aniane granted property to "Guillermo domini Montispessulani filio quondam Ma
2 CONC thilde ducisse" by charter dated 12 Jun 1202[865].  In his testament dated 4 Nov 1202, he bequeathed Montpellier to "Guillelmum filium meum primogenitum", Tortosa to "Thome filio meo qui dicitur Tortosa", left money for "Raimundum
2 CONC  filium meum" to become a monk at "Grandis-sylvæ", for "Bernardium Guillelmum filium meum" to become a canon "Girundæ et Lodoicensem", for "Guidonem filium meum" to become a monk at Cluny, for "filium meum Burgundionem" to become 
2 CONC a canon "de Podio", left money to "filiæ meæ Mariæ…et comes Convenarum maritus eius", left money to "filiabus meis Agneti et Adalaiz", and named "Agnes uxor mea" and "patruus meus Guido…fratris mei Bergundionis…Clementiæ sorori me
2 CONC æ…viro suo Rostagno de Sabino"[866].  The Thalamus de Montpellier records the death in Sep 1203 of "G. de Montpellier"[867]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' firstly ([1180], divorced Apr 1187) EVDOKIA [Komnene], daughter of --- ([1160/64]-[Nov 1202/Jun 1204]).  ... ... ... ...
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' secondly (Barcelona Apr 1187, bigamously) doña INÉS, daughter of --- (-after 4 Nov 1202).  ... ...
2 CONT
2 CONT  Guillaume [VIII] & his first wife had one child:
2 CONT
2 CONT  Guillaume [VIII] & his second wife had eight (illegitimate) children:
2 CONT
2 CONT --------
2 CONT --------
2 CONT -------
2 CONT
2 CONT William VIII of Montpellier (died 1202) was Lord of Montpellier, the son of William VII.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Eudoxie or Eudokia Komnene, niece of the Byzantine emperor Manuel I Komnenos. A condition of the marriage was that the firstborn child, boy or girl, would succeed to the lordship of Montpellier on William's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wiiliam VIII was a patron of troubadours. Arnaut de Mareuil came to his court after fleeing from the entourage of Azalais of Toulouse, and at least one of Arnaut's poems is addressed to him.
2 CONT
2 CONT William died in 1202. He and Eudoxie had no sons, and it was their daughter Marie of Montpellier who was to benefit from the terms of the marriage agreement.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_VIII_of_Montpellier
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT William VIII of Montpellier (died 1202) was Lord of Montpellier, the son of William VII.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Eudoxie or Eudokia Komnene, grand-niece of the Byzantine emperor Manuel I Komnenos. A condition of the marriage was that the firstborn child, boy or girl, would succeed to the lordship of Montpellier on William's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wiiliam VIII was a patron of troubadours. Arnaut de Mareuil came to his court after fleeing from the entourage of Azalais of Toulouse, and at least one of Arnaut's poems is addressed to him.
2 CONT
2 CONT William died in 1202. He and Eudoxie had no sons, and it was their daughter Marie of Montpellier who was to benefit from the terms of the marriage agreement.
