1 NAME Fernando /Afonso de Albuquerque/
2 GIVN Fernando
2 SURN Afonso de Albuquerque
1 NAME Fernando Afonso de Albuquerque, alcaide da Guarda
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1327
2 ADDR
3 CTRY Portugal
1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT 1387
1 OCCU Ambassador & Master of the Portuguese military order of St James // Embaixador & Mestre da Ordem de Santiago
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Fernando Afonso de Albuquerque. Ambassador & Master of the Portuguese military order of St James.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''''''Treaty of Windsor (1386)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Negotiators (Diplomats):''' 
2 CONT 1- '''Fernando Afonso de Albuquerque''' also known as '''Ferdinand Afonso de Albuquerque''';
2 CONT 2- '''Lourenço João Fogaça''' also known as '''Lawrence John Fogaça''';
2 CONT 3- '''Richard d'Alberbury''';
2 CONT 4- '''John Clanowe''';
2 CONT 5- '''Richard Ronhale'''.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT *
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The Treaty of Windsor was a '''diplomatic alliance''' signed between Portugal and England on May 9, 1386, in Windsor and sealed by the marriage of King John I of Portugal (House of Aviz) to Philippa of Lancaster, daughter of John 
2 CONC of Gaunt, 1st Duke of Lancaster. With the victory at the Battle of Aljubarrota, assisted by English archers, John I was recognised as the undisputed King of Portugal, putting an end to the interregnum of the 1383–1385 Crisis. The 
2 CONC Treaty of Windsor established a pact of mutual support between the countries.
2 CONT
2 CONT This document is preserved at the Portuguese National Archives.
2 CONT
2 CONT Historian Matthew Winslett says, "This treaty has been the cornerstone of both nations' relations with each other ever since."
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Enduring to this day, the Treaty of Windsor holds the record as the longest-lasting diplomatic treaty in recorded history.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT in Wikipedia (English)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT *
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT "'''Winston Churchill in a speech in the House of Commons in October 1943 famously described the unique and ancient friendship between England and Portugal as an alliance “without parallel in world history”.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT It is 630 years since '''a treaty of alliance between King Richard II of England and King John I of Portugal was ratified at Windsor on the 9th May 1386''' (The National Archives: E 30/310). Though it is not the earliest of the An
2 CONC glo-Portuguese treaties that survive, the Treaty of Windsor is significant because it effectively cemented and strengthened ties between the two kingdoms and helped convert a fledgling alliance into a more permanent legacy of hist
2 CONC ory.
2 CONT
2 CONT The terms of the treaty included provisions for guaranteeing the mutual security of both nations and strengthening of commercial ties such as the right of both countries to trade on the terms enjoyed by the subjects of that countr
2 CONC y. There were even clauses encouraging freedom of movement and settlement between the two countries, as subjects of either country had the right to dwell in the domains of the other.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The seals attached''' to the document '''are those of the Portuguese ambassadors: Ferdinand Afonso de Albuquerque''', '''Master of the Portuguese military order of St James''' and '''Chancellor of Portugal Lawrence John Fogaca'
2 CONC '' entrusted as plenipotentiaries in the treaty negotiations."
2 CONT
2 CONT in https://history.blog.gov.uk/2016/05/09/historys-unparalleled-alliance-the-anglo-portuguese-treaty-of-windsor-9th-may-1386/
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT *
