1 CONC ounger son Berenguer Ramon. Ramon Berenguer also had a daughter Berengaria (d.1147),who married Alfonso VII of Castile .
0 @N6000000002187750329@ NOTE Ramon Berenguer III, Count of Barcelona
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ramon Berenguer III the Great was the count of Barcelona, Girona, and Osona from 1082 (jointly with Berenguer Ramon II and solely from 1097), Besalú from 1111, Cerdagne from 1117, and Provence, in the Holy Roman Empire, from 1112,
1 CONC  all until his death in Barcelona in 1131
1 CONT
1 CONT Born in 1082 in Rodez, he was the son of Ramon Berenguer II. He succeeded his father to co-rule with his uncle Berenguer Ramon II. He became the sole ruler in 1097, when Berenguer Ramon II was forced into exile,
1 CONT
1 CONT During his rule Catalan interests were extended on both sides of the Pyrenees. By marriage or vassalage he incorporated into his realm almost all of the Catalan counties (except those of Urgell and Peralada). He inherited the coun
1 CONC ties of Besalú (1111) and Cerdagne (1117) and in between married Douce, heiress of Provence (1112). His dominions then stretched as far east as Nice.
1 CONT
1 CONT In alliance with the Count of Urgell, Ramon Berenguer conquered Barbastro and Balaguer. In 1118 he captured and rebuilt Tarragona, which became the metropolitan seat of the church in Catalonia (before that, Catalans had depended e
1 CONC cclesiastically on the archbishopric of Narbonne). He also established relations with the Italian maritime republics of Pisa and Genoa and in 1114 and 1115 raided with them the Moorish pirate strongholds of Majorca and Ibiza. They
1 CONC  became his tributaries and many Christian slaves there were recovered and set free. Ramon Berenguer also raided mainland Muslim dependencies with Pisa's help, such as Valencia, Lleida and Tortosa.
1 CONT
1 CONT Toward the end of his life Ramon Berenguer became a Templar. He gave his five Catalonian counties to his eldest son Ramon Berenguer IV and Provence to the younger son Berenguer Ramon.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Ramon Berenguer's marriages and descendants
1 CONT First wife, María Rodríguez de Vivar, second daughter of Cid, died ca. 1105 
1 CONT María -> married Bernat III, Count of Besalú (d. 1111) 
1 CONT Jimena, a.k.a. Eixemena -> married Roger III, Count of Foix 
1 CONT Second wife, Almodis 
1 CONT Third wife, Douce or Dolça de Gévaudaun, heiress of Provence, d. ca. 11
1 CONT Almodis -> married Ponce de Cervera 
1 CONT Berenguela or Berengaria, b. 1108, d. 1149 -> married Alfonso VII of Castile 
1 CONT Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Barcelona, b. 1115, d. 1162 
1 CONT Berenguer Ramon I, Count of Provence, b. ca. 1115, d. 1144 
1 CONT Bernat -> died young 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ramon Berenguer II and
1 CONT Berenguer Ramon II Count of Barcelona
1 CONT with Berenguer Ramon II Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ramon Berenguer IV 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Douce Count of Provence Succeeded by:
1 CONT Berenguer Ramon I 
0 @N6000000003051138477@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Ramon Berenguer III, Count of Barcelona", retrieved 15 Oct 07]
1 CONT Ramon Berenguer III the Great was the count of Barcelona, Girona, and Osona from 1082 (jointly with Berenguer Ramon II and solely from 1097), Besalú from 1111, Cerdanya from 1117, and Provence, in the Holy Roman Empire, from 1112,
1 CONC  all until his death in Barcelona in 1131. As Ramon Berenguer I, he was Count of Provence from 1112 in right of his wife.
1 CONT
1 CONT Born in 1082 in Rodez, he was the son of Ramon Berenguer II. He succeeded his father to co-rule with his uncle Berenguer Ramon II. He became the sole ruler in 1097, when Berenguer Ramon II was forced into exile.
1 CONT
1 CONT During his rule Catalan interests were extended on both sides of the Pyrenees. By marriage or vassalage he incorporated into his realm almost all of the Catalan counties (except those of Urgell and Peralada). He inherited the coun
1 CONC ties of Besalú (1111) and Cerdanya (1117) and in between married Douce, heiress of Provence (1112). His dominions then stretched as far east as Nice.
1 CONT
1 CONT In alliance with the Count of Urgell, Ramon Berenguer conquered Barbastro and Balaguer. In 1118 he captured and rebuilt Tarragona, which became the metropolitan seat of the church in Catalonia (before that, Catalans had depended e
1 CONC cclesiastically on the archbishopric of Narbonne). He also established relations with the Italian maritime republics of Pisa and Genoa and in 1114 and 1115 raided with them the Moorish pirate strongholds of Majorca and Ibiza. They
1 CONC  became his tributaries and many Christian slaves there were recovered and set free. Ramon Berenguer also raided mainland Muslim dependencies with Pisa's help, such as Valencia, Lleida and Tortosa.
1 CONT
1 CONT Toward the end of his life Ramon Berenguer became a Templar. He gave his five Catalonian counties to his eldest son Ramon Berenguer IV and Provence to the younger son Berenguer Ramon.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ramon Berenguer's marriages and descendant
1 CONT - First wife, María Rodríguez de Vivar, second daughter of Cid, died ca. 1105
1 CONT - María -> married Bernat III, Count of Besalú (d. 1111)
1 CONT - Jimena, a.k.a. Eixemena -> married Roger III, Count of Foix
1 CONT - Second wife, Almodis
1 CONT - Third wife, Douce or Dolça de Gévaudaun, heiress of Provence, d. ca. 1127
1 CONT - Almodis -> married Ponce de Cervera, mother of Agalbursa, who married Barisone II of Arborea
1 CONT - Berenguela or Berengaria, b. 1116, d. 1149 -> married Alfonso VII of Castile
1 CONT - Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Barcelona, b. 1115, d. 1162
1 CONT - Berenguer Ramon I, Count of Provence, b. ca. 1115, d. 1144
1 CONT - Bernat -> died young
0 @N6000000003637312905@ NOTE En primeras nupcias desposó a María Rodríguez, hija del Cid Campeador.1 Casó en segundas nupcias con Dulce de Provenza o de Rouergue, con quien tuvo en 1108 a Berenguela de Barcelona, esposa del Rey Alfonso VII de Castilla y a los
1 CONC  gemelos Ramón Berenguer IV y Berenguer Ramón I de Provenza, en 1114.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ingresó en la Orden de los Templarios como última voluntad, estando ya en su lecho de muerte, en julio de 1131. Investido por Hugo de Rigaud, murió cinco días después y fue enterrado con el hábito blanco del Temple. En su testamen
1 CONC to legó a la Orden su caballo, de nombre Danc, y sus armas personales, así como el castillo de Grañena.
1 CONT
1 CONT Su hijo Ramón Berenguer IV heredó el condado de Barcelona en (1131), Berenguer Ramón el Condado de Provenza y su hija Jimena casó con Roger III de Foix.
1 CONT
1 CONT En la plaza de Barcelona que lleva su nombre, sobre la Vía Layetana, hay una estatua ecuestre suya obra del escultor Josep Llimona.
1 CONT
1 CONT Conde de Barcelona y Girona (1097-1131)
1 CONT Conde de Osona (1097-1107 y 1111-1131)
1 CONT Conde de Provenza (1113-1131)
1 CONT Conde de Cerdaña (1118-1131).
1 CONT Primer Caballero Templario de la Península Ibérica.
0 @N6000000003949866982@ NOTE byname RAMON BERENGUER THE GREAT, Catalan RAMON BERENGUER EL GRAN (b. 1082--d. 1131, Barcelona [Spain]), count of Barcelona during whose reign, (1097-1131) independent Catalonia reached the summit of its historical greatness, spre
1 CONC ading its ships over the western Mediterranean and acquiring new lands from the southern Pyrennees to Provence. He was also known as Ramon Berenguer I of Provence.   The son of Ramon Berenguer II, he took the throne on the departu
1 CONC re of his uncle, Berenguer Ramon II, on crusade and spent his early years fighting off Almoravid Muslims, whose armies approached the very walls of Barcelona. Thereafter, his expansionist campaigns began. In 1111 he conquered the 
1 CONC county of Besalú and, by his marriage to Douce (or Dolça) of Provence in 1112, acquired the county of Provence. In the years 1114-15 he undertook, with the Pisans, a joint expedition against the Balearic Islands, liberating thousa
1 CONC nds of Christian slaves and destroying the Moors' piratical bases. Commerce thereafter flourished between Barcelona, Marseille, Genoa, and Pisa. The following year (1116) he sailed to Rome in an attempt to gain aid from the Italia
1 CONC n states and to acquire a license from the Pope for his crusade in Spain, but the visit was largely unsuccessful. In 1117 he inherited the old county of Cerdaña in the Pyrenees.
1 CONT On his death, Provence went to his younger son, Berenguer Ramon (as Berenguer Ramon I of Provence, reigning 1131-44); and the rest of the lands, the most important ones, went to the elder son, Ramon Berenguer IV. [Encyclopaedia Br
1 CONC itannica CD '97]
