1 CONC e, Holy Roman Empire, from 1112. Born in 1082 in Rodez, he was the son of Ramon BerengerII. He succeeded his father to co-rule with his uncle Berenguer Ramon II. After 1097, when Berenguer Ramon II was forced into exile, he was th
1 CONC e sole ruler. During his rule Catalan interests were extended on both sides of the Pyrenees. By marriage or vassalage he incorporated into his realm almost all of the Catalan counties (except those of Urgell and Peralada). He inhe
1 CONC rited the counties of Besalú (1111) and Cerdagne (1117) and in between married Douce, heiress of Provence (1112). Now his dominions stretched as far east as Nice. In alliance with the Count of Urgell Ramon Berenguer conquered Barb
1 CONC astro and Balaguer. In 1118 he captured and rebuilt Tarragona, which became the metropolitan seat of the church in Catalonia (before that, Catalans had depended ecclesiastically on the archbishopric of Narbonne). He also establish
1 CONC ed relations with the Italian maritime republics of Pisa and Genoa and in 1114-1115, raided with them the Moorish pirate strongholds of Majorca and Ibiza. They became his tributaries and many Christian slaves were recovered and se
1 CONC t free. Jointly with Pisa Ramon Berenguer also raided Muslim dependencies on the mainland, including lands of Valencia, Lleida and Tortosa. Toward the end of his life Ramon Berenguer became a Templar. He gave his five Catalonian c
1 CONC ounties to his eldest son Ramon Berenguer IV and Provence to the younger son Berenguer Ramon.
